  # AI Will Reshape More Jobs Than It Replaces
  
  By [Greg Emerson](https://www.bcg.com/about/people/experts/greg-emerson), [Matthew Kropp](https://www.bcg.com/about/people/experts/matthew-kropp), [Julie Bedard](https://www.bcg.com/about/people/experts/julie-bedard), [Lisa Krayer](https://www.bcg.com/about/people/experts/lisa-krayer), Viacheslav Romanov, Megan Hsu, Luis Sanchez Boedo, and Diya Mohnot
  
  ArticleApril 03, 202615 MIN read
  
  
  
  ![](https://web-assets.bcg.com/dims4/default/e3ad245/2147483647/strip/true/crop/2880x1620+0+0/resize/768x432!/format/webp/quality/90/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fboston-consulting-group-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F01%2F6b%2F6458a17e4f19b3f8b0ce0f2909ca%2Fai-will-reshape-more-jobs-than-it-replaces-hero.jpg)
  
  ## Key Takeaways
  
  Task automation doesn’t equal job loss. Most roles will remain—but will change substantially.
  
  - Our microeconomic model reveals that over the next two to three years, 50% to 55% of jobs in the US will be reshaped by AI.
  - Workforce strategy cannot sit downstream of automation. It must be embedded in strategy.
  - For CEOs, the imperative is to focus on achieving the right balance of automation, upskilling, and deliberate talent planning
  
