  ## Crucial Quote
  
  “Companies are shifting budgets toward AI investments at the expense of jobs,” said Andy Challenger, workplace expert and chief revenue officer at career services firm Challenger, Gray & Christmas. “The actual replacing of roles can be seen in technology companies, where AI can replace coding functions. Other industries are testing the limits of this new technology, and while it can’t replace jobs completely, it is costing jobs.”
  
  ## Big Number
  
  30,000. That’s how many layoffs have been blamed on AI so far this year, according to Challenger. AI was cited for almost 55,000 cuts in 2025.
  
  ## Key Background
  
  Tech CEOs have recently warned middle management and white collar jobs are likely to be the most vulnerable in the AI renaissance. Billionaire [Dario Amodei,](https://www.axios.com/2025/05/28/ai-jobs-white-collar-unemployment-anthropic "https://www.axios.com/2025/05/28/ai-jobs-white-collar-unemployment-anthropic") founder and CEO of AI giant Anthropic, last year said artificial intelligence could wipe out half of all entry-level white-collar jobs and send unemployment soaring. He accused AI companies and government officials of "sugar-coating" the reality that mass job eliminations are likely coming in technology, finance, law and other sectors. Dorsey and former Sequoia managing partner Roelof Botha [last month said](https://www.forbes.com/sites/brandonkochkodin/2026/03/31/billionaire-jack-dorsey-thinks-ai-will-kill-middle-management/ "https://www.forbes.com/sites/brandonkochkodin/2026/03/31/billionaire-jack-dorsey-thinks-ai-will-kill-middle-management/") they think AI can do much of what middle managers, or about 12% of the workforce, do today. [Just Capital](https://justcapital.com/news/new-research-reveals-how-public-expectations-on-ai-are-shifting/ "https://justcapital.com/news/new-research-reveals-how-public-expectations-on-ai-are-shifting/"), a nonprofit that conducts business-related polling, this week said one-third of the public worries about significant layoffs due to AI displacing roles. [More than half](https://justcapital.com/news/new-research-reveals-how-public-expectations-on-ai-are-shifting/ "https://justcapital.com/news/new-research-reveals-how-public-expectations-on-ai-are-shifting/") of corporate leaders polled said they think hiring will slow for entry-level positions in the coming years, and that remaining jobs will require employees to be more skilled than before. Other analysis [suggests](https://www.economist.com/finance-and-economics/2026/04/13/the-tech-jobs-bust-is-real-dont-blame-ai-yet "https://www.economist.com/finance-and-economics/2026/04/13/the-tech-jobs-bust-is-real-dont-blame-ai-yet") tech jobs are much more at risk than roles in other sectors. America’s Census Bureau [estimates](https://www2.census.gov/ces/wp/2023/CES-WP-23-48.pdf "https://www2.census.gov/ces/wp/2023/CES-WP-23-48.pdf") that companies in tech-heavy areas like San Francisco, Boston and Seattle are using AI at a much higher rate than the rest of the country.
  
