  #### **84% of employees worldwide are receiving significant organizational support to learn AI skills ( [McKinsey](https://www.mckinsey.com/capabilities/mckinsey-digital/our-insights/superagency-in-the-workplace-empowering-people-to-unlock-ais-full-potential-at-work))**
  
  **![Chart showing the prevalence of AI training in organizations worldwide](https://cdn.buttercms.com/output=f:webp/7vvHKzjfTzS0JxP4A18W)**
  
  Employers who fail to train their workers in AI may find themselves falling behind their competitors. That could drive this statistic higher in coming years.
  
  #### **Half of all US workers receive little or no AI training from employers ( [Exploding Topics](https://explodingtopics.com/blog/ai-workforce-research))**
  
  **![ai-workforce-survey-training-or-support-min.png](https://cdn.buttercms.com/output=f:webp/dluRgMQrQbuhTsa49SWX)**
  
  According to our original research, 50.96% of workers who use AI have received (at most) limited training. Almost 1 in 5 (19.5%) have received no support at all.
  
  #### **48% of US workers want more formal Gen AI training from their organization (** **[McKinsey](https://www.mckinsey.com/capabilities/mckinsey-digital/our-insights/superagency-in-the-workplace-empowering-people-to-unlock-ais-full-potential-at-work))**
  
  Training is the single biggest factor that would see more employees integrate AI into their daily work routines.
  
  #### **AI is the highest priority in the training strategies of companies with more than 50,000 employees ( [WEF](https://www3.weforum.org/docs/WEF_Future_of_Jobs_2023.pdf))**
  
  Larger companies are more likely to embrace and fully adopt AI tools, which naturally means more of them will have to make AI training a top priority. Various industries also have AI as the highest priority of their training strategies, including IT services, media and entertainment, telecommunications, and electronics.
  
  ### AI Causing Widespread Worry Among Workers
  
  #### **32% of US workers fear that AI will lead to fewer job opportunities ( [Pew](https://www.pewresearch.org/social-trends/2025/02/25/u-s-workers-are-more-worried-than-hopeful-about-future-ai-use-in-the-workplace/))**
  
  All in all, 52% of workers are worried by AI, compared to just 36% who are hopeful. Only 29% feel excited, while 33% feel overwhelmed.
  
  #### **39% of workers are concerned they won’t receive adequate training in new technology ( [PwC](https://www.pwc.com/gx/en/issues/workforce/hopes-and-fears-2022.html))**
  
  Keeping up with technological change is vital if you want to stay afloat in an increasingly competitive workforce. A significant proportion of workers are worried they won’t receive the training necessary to properly use new tech, including AI tools. The same PwC survey found that employees who believe their skills are scarce are far more likely to ask for a pay rise or promotion, feel satisfied with their job, and switch to a new employer.
  
  #### **43% of workers fear that using AI at work will make their job seem obsolete ( [Exploding Topics](https://explodingtopics.com/blog/ai-workforce-research\#will-ai-take-your-job-we-asked-an-expert))**
  
  Almost half of all workers list the risk of looking replaceable as a major concern about using AI at work.
  
  #### **43% of workers expect AI to cause their job to significantly change in the next five years ( [Survey Monkey](https://www.surveymonkey.com/curiosity/cnbc-workforce-survey-may-2023/))**
  
  This isn’t necessarily bad. However, significant job changes often result in increased pressure and stress due to the need to adapt, especially if an employer does not provide retraining.
  
  #### **Just 32% of employees think their company has been transparent about its use of AI ( [Asana](https://asana.com/work-innovation-lab/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/The-State-of-AI-at-Work.pdf))**
  
  Perhaps unsurprisingly, workers are less likely to think this if they are lower down in the business hierarchy. While 44% of executives thought their company has been transparent about its [AI usage](https://explodingtopics.com/blog/ai-usage-statistics), only 38% of managers agreed, and that figure fell to 25% among individual contributors.
  
  #### **55% of workers think AI will eliminate more jobs than it will create ( [BMG](https://inews.co.uk/news/politics/ai-fear-jobs-uk-public-robots-act-against-human-interests-2385893))**
  
  ![undefined](https://cdn.buttercms.com/output=f:webp/pbx5hfoaRsCIYqMHvHON)
  
  Despite reassurances from leaders in the AI space, people are still wary of this rapidly evolving technology. Just under half of respondents to the same poll said they thought it was likely that humans would eventually lose control of AI systems altogether.
  
  #### **US unemployment could reach 20% in the next five years ( [CNN](https://edition.cnn.com/2025/05/29/tech/ai-anthropic-ceo-dario-amodei-unemployment))**
  
  That’s the warning from the Anthropic CEO. It echoes fears that AI will not create as many jobs as it displaces.
  
  ### Who is Most Worried About AI Replacing Their Job
  
  #### **81.6% of digital marketers think that content writers will lose jobs due to AI ( [SEO.ai](https://seo.ai/blog/ai-replacing-jobs-statistics))**
  
  ![undefined](https://cdn.buttercms.com/output=f:webp/RHagU7IR6Sx9BTUa348K)
  
  Content production has been one of the industries most impacted by AI, so it’s no surprise the vast majority of those involved in content creation predict jobs will be lost.
  
  #### **52% of people aged 18-24 are worried that AI will negatively impact their future careers ( [BMG](https://inews.co.uk/news/politics/ai-fear-jobs-uk-public-robots-act-against-human-interests-2385893))**
  
  Young workers have the most time ahead of them — time in which AI tools can become even more advanced, and pose even more of a challenge to human workers. For context, just 39% of all respondents in this poll indicated concern about their future career prospects being negatively impacted by AI.
  
  #### **Young workers are 129% more likely to be worried than older workers about AI making their job obsolete ( [SurveyMonkey](https://www.surveymonkey.com/curiosity/cnbc-workforce-survey-may-2023/))**
  
  While 32% of workers aged 18-24 reported being somewhat or very worried that AI will make their job obsolete soon, only 14% of workers aged 65 and over felt worried. Each intermediate age group felt more worried the younger they were, perhaps reflecting the face that younger workers will likely have to compete against successively more powerful AI models.
  
  #### **38% of Asian workers are worried AI will make their job obsolete, compared to just 19% of white workers ( [SurveyMonkey](https://www.surveymonkey.com/curiosity/cnbc-workforce-survey-may-2023/))**
  
  Asian workers were the most worried, while white workers were the least afraid. In the middle, 32% of Black workers and 35% of Hispanic workers reported feeling concerned about AI making their job obsolete.
  
  #### **Fully remote workers are 42% more likely than fully in-person employees to believe their job will be disrupted by AI ( [SurveyMonkey](https://www.surveymonkey.com/curiosity/cnbc-workforce-survey-may-2023/))**
  
  While 54% of fully remote workers think that their job will likely be changed by AI, only 38% of employees who work fully in-person think the same.
  
  #### **51% of workers in advertising and marketing believe their jobs will change due to AI ( [SurveyMonkey](https://www.surveymonkey.com/curiosity/cnbc-workforce-survey-may-2023/))**
  
  ![undefined](https://cdn.buttercms.com/output=f:webp/v31yQ1oQF2ls2lbxKUxQ)
  
  This represents one of the highest proportions of workers predicting job changes of any industry. AI tools are already being used by some companies to promote their products.
  
  ### Where AI is Being Adopted
  
  #### **Companies with annual revenue of at least $500 million are adopting AI more quickly than smaller organizations ( [McKinsey](https://www.mckinsey.com/capabilities/quantumblack/our-insights/the-state-of-ai))**
  
  Adopting AI comes with often significant costs. Larger organizations that can absorb these upfront expenses are thus more able and likely to adopt AI tools. According to the 2025 State of AI report, these larger companies tend to use AI in more areas of the business.
  
  #### **More than 75% of all organizations now use AI in at least one business function ( [McKinsey](https://www.mckinsey.com/capabilities/quantumblack/our-insights/the-state-of-ai))**
  
  Larger companies are adapting more quickly, but AI adoption is already cutting across the majority of industries and businesses.
  
  ### AI’s Potential to Create New Careers
  
  #### **81% of office workers hold a generally favorable view of AI ( [SnapLogic](https://www.snaplogic.com/resources/infographics/employees-want-more-ai-in-the-workplace))**
  
  ![undefined](https://cdn.buttercms.com/output=f:webp/i8BemyMGRmSq4ZOfBzz8)
  
  AI might be taking some of their jobs, but office workers are still overwhelmingly positive about the new technology. More than four-fifths believe it improves their job performance and experience at work.
  
  #### **51% of office workers say AI lets them achieve better work-life balance ( [SnapLogic](https://www.snaplogic.com/resources/infographics/employees-want-more-ai-in-the-workplace))**
  
  The utopian promise of AI, spread by major proponents like Elon Musk, is a world where work isn’t necessary at all. For the minute, however, work remains one part of the work-life balance. A majority of workers are using AI to push the scale further toward “life.”
  
  #### **Alternatively, just 6% of workers say AI will lead to more job opportunities in the long run ( [Pew](https://www.pewresearch.org/social-trends/2025/02/25/u-s-workers-are-more-worried-than-hopeful-about-future-ai-use-in-the-workplace/))**
  
  31% predict the technology won't make much difference, while 32% think it will lead to job losses.
  
  #### **Among those using AI chatbots at work, 79% say it allows them to do things at least somewhat more quickly ( [Pew](https://www.pewresearch.org/social-trends/2025/02/25/u-s-workers-are-more-worried-than-hopeful-about-future-ai-use-in-the-workplace/))**
  
  40% answered extremely/very. Only 29% said that AI chatbots had a similarly major positive impact on the quality of their work.
  
  #### **Between 2025 and 2030, the profession with the largest net job growth worldwide will be “Big Data Specialist” ( [WEF](https://reports.weforum.org/docs/WEF_Future_of_Jobs_Report_2025.pdf))**
  
  Data specialists, FinTech engineers, AI specialists, and data warehousing specialists can all expect improved job prospects in the near future.
  
  #### **Having access to an LLM resulted in 15% of employee tasks being completed faster with no loss in quality ( [ARXIV](https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.10130))**
  
  By allowing employees to complete more work in the same amount of time, AI tools might induce employers to cut back on workers.
  
  ### Miscellaneous Stats About AI Replacing Jobs
  
  #### **26% of workers think they are considered lazy if they use AI ( [Asana](https://asana.com/work-innovation-lab/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/The-State-of-AI-at-Work.pdf))**
  
  ![undefined](https://cdn.buttercms.com/output=f:webp/lBtQ4VXBRACT5FuH9VnX)
  
  While the vast majority of workers aren’t worried about using AI tools, a significant proportion worry it could negatively impact how people view their work ethic. That’s not an unreasonable concern, as being considered lazy could mean being passed up for a pay rise or promotion.
  
  #### **18% of workers feel like a fraud when they use AI for work ( [Asana](https://asana.com/work-innovation-lab/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/The-State-of-AI-at-Work.pdf))**
  
  Debates around the proper use of AI continue. The direction of popular culture will have a significant impact on this statistic. Will people accept the use of AI for work, or make it unacceptable?
  
  #### **15% of US workers are open to having an AI boss ( [Asana](https://asana.com/work-innovation-lab/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/The-State-of-AI-at-Work.pdf))**
  
  ![undefined](https://cdn.buttercms.com/output=f:webp/G16FOgyYS9CM30JgtrYP)
  
  Americans are apparently more receptive to the idea of having a robot boss than British workers. Just 8% of workers in the UK said they’d be open to this idea.
  
  #### **One-third of all US jobs created in the last quarter-century didn’t exist before ( [McKinsey](https://www.mckinsey.com/featured-insights/employment-and-growth/technology-jobs-and-the-future-of-work))**
  
  Technically, these jobs either didn’t exist or “barely existed.” Technological advancements can eliminate jobs, but they often create entirely new professions that no one could have predicted.
  
  ### Conclusion
  
  With AI still advancing so rapidly, even experts are in disagreement about the exact effect this new technology will have on global employment.
  
  On the one hand, AI may well lead to widespread job losses. However, it’s already improving the work lives of millions of employees.
  
  On the other hand, AI is set to create hundreds of thousands of new jobs. And yet millions of people have already been replaced by robots.
  
  Regardless, as AI continues to advance and be adopted, its impact on the global workforce (both positive and negative) will only increase.
  
